<p>Today's brief covers stories from 9 to 11 November.</p>

<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Connectors</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Mittwald MCP server<em>entry point to customer projects</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Inference</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">NEAR AI<em>SGLang flaw inside the enclave</em></span><span class="hl">Fireworks AI<em>second breach via a cloud provider</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Capacity and capital</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Rackspace and Together AI<em>GB300 for 2027</em></span><span>Anthropic IPO<em>price range set</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Attackers are working through every layer of the AI stack, from connectors to inference servers.</figcaption>
</figure>

<section class="block">
<h2>1. NEAR AI breached through a flaw in the SGLang inference engine</h2>
<p>NEAR AI disclosed that an attacker used a remote code execution flaw in SGLang, a widely used open-source inference engine, to run code on one of its inference servers<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. The Cloud Security Alliance notes that the attacker could observe in-flight requests on that server, including ones meant to be protected by confidential computing, because the enclave protects against the host and operator but not against flaws in software running inside it<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. NEAR AI said it revoked credentials and deployed a fix across its inference servers<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> confidential computing is a strong control, but it does not patch the software inside the enclave. If you self-host inference with SGLang, vLLM or similar engines, treat them like any internet-facing service: keep them patched, never expose their internal ports, and restrict what the serving process can reach. If you buy confidential inference, ask providers for their patch cadence and egress controls, not just their attestation. See <a href="/insights/ai-model-supply-chain-security/">AI model supply chain security</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>2. Fireworks AI discloses a second breach</h2>
<p>Fireworks AI told customers that an October intrusion through a third-party cloud provider reached its development machines, exposing account and billing information and, for a limited set of accounts, secrets, datasets and incomplete model weights<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. It is the company's second disclosed incident in weeks; it has restricted some data upload and download functions while it investigates<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> any provider you send fine-tuning data or keys to is holding your crown jewels. Rotate any secrets stored with affected providers, review what datasets you have uploaded and whether they are still needed, and keep the ability to move inference to another provider at short notice. See <a href="/insights/llm-model-routing/">model routing</a> and the <a href="/case-studies/japan-securities-third-party-cloud-access/">third-party access case study</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>3. A web host is breached through its MCP server</h2>
<p>German hosting provider Mittwald said an attacker exploited a flaw in its MCP server, which connects customers' AI assistants to its mStudio platform, and accessed 56 customer projects before being detected; it shut down the server, revoked session tokens and asked users to change passwords and review API keys<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. Whether the flaw was in Mittwald's own code or an underlying component has not been confirmed<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> an MCP server that can act on customer resources is a privileged API and needs the same rigour: strong authentication, narrow scopes per user, rate limits, and logging that would spot a stranger moving between customers. If you publish an MCP server, review it this week. See the <a href="/insights/securing-mcp-servers-checklist/">MCP server security checklist</a> and the <a href="/fractional-cto-europe/">Europe page</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>4. Rackspace signs a GB300 capacity deal with Together AI</h2>
<p>Rackspace announced a multi-year agreement to provide Together AI with dedicated Nvidia GB300 NVL72 infrastructure, valued at about $380 million and expected in service by mid-2027; Converge Digest notes it remains conditional on financing and on equipment, power and facility availability<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> new inference capacity is being contracted now for 2027, with financing still to be arranged. If you depend on a specialist inference provider, ask where its future capacity is coming from and how firm those plans are. See <a href="/insights/microsoft-oracle-gpu-capacity-contracts/">AI capacity contracts</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>5. Anthropic sets its IPO price range</h2>
<p>Anthropic set a price range of $70 to $78 per share for its IPO, and people familiar with the plans told CNBC it aims to start trading on 19 November<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> for customers, little changes on listing day. Over time, public-market pressure can shape pricing, product priorities and disclosure. Keep provider choice open and contracts flexible, as with any strategic supplier.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd do this week</h2>
<ul>
<li>Patch self-hosted inference engines and check their ports are not exposed.</li>
<li>Rotate secrets held by AI providers that have disclosed incidents.</li>
<li>Review any MCP servers you publish for authentication, scoping and logging.</li>
<li>Ask inference providers about their capacity plans for 2027.</li>
</ul>
<p>I am based in India, the United States and the UAE, working globally. See <a href="/regions/">where I work</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
